WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 14: The Oklahoma Land Rush of 1889: High Noon in the Unassigned Lands

1
00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:05.040
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:06.240
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:10.960
[Rebecca] In the 19th century, the promise of free land was the great American dream.

4
00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:18.400
[Rebecca] Nowhere was that dream realized in a more chaotic, frenzied and dangerous spectacle than on the open prairie of Oklahoma.

5
00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:28.080
[Rebecca] On April 22nd, 1889, the federal government opened two million acres of prime frontier land to settlement under the Homestead Act.

6
00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:33.920
[Rebecca] But instead of an orderly lottery or auction, the government chose a contest of raw speed.

7
00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:40.320
[Rebecca] A massive, literal foothand horse race where whoever drove a wooden stake into the ground first on the land.

8
00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:46.160
[Rebecca] 50,000 desperate pioneers lined the border, waiting for the sound of a bugle at high noon.

9
00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:49.520
[George] Here is George with the story.

10
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:09.120
[George] Covering nearly two million acres of central Oklahoma, this sea of tall grass prairie, rolling post oak ridges, and clear river bottoms had a complex and tragic history.

11
00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:27.360
[George] Decades earlier, following the forced displacement of the Trail of Tears under the Indian Removal Act of 1830, the federal government had deeded these lands to the Creek and Seminole nations, promising in sacred treaties that the soil would belong to them as long as grass grows and water runs.

12
00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:40.160
[George] However, following the Civil War, the federal government forced the tribes to sign the Reconstruction Treaties of 1866, ceding the Central Tract as punishment for factional alliances with the Confederacy.

13
00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:50.480
[George] Intended originally for the relocation of other tribes, the two million acre tract was never formally allocated, leaving it an empty island in the middle of Indian territory.

14
00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:57.680
[George] For over a decade, landless white homesteaders, known as boomers and led by charismatic agitators like David L.

15
00:01:57.680 --> 00:01:58.800
[George] Payne and William L.

16
00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:04.360
[George] Couch, had illegally crossed the border from Kansas to establish unauthorized settlements.

17
00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:13.400
[George] Each time, the United States cavalry had hunted them down, burned their makeshift cabins, and escorted them back across the Kansas line.

18
00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:24.680
[George] Yet, political pressure from commercial land syndicates, railroad corporations seeking freight traffic, and impoverished frontier families became completely irresistible in Washington.

19
00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:33.880
[George] Finally, in March of 1889, the United States Congress attached an amendment to the Indian Appropriations Bill, authorizing settlement.

20
00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:39.800
[George] On March 23, President Benjamin Harrison signed a historic executive proclamation.

21
00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:49.640
[George] At precisely 12 o'clock noon on Monday, April 22, 1889, the unassigned lands would be opened to white settlement under the Homestead Act.

22
00:02:49.640 --> 00:03:05.480
[George] Any legal citizen or immigrant who had declared their intention to become one could claim 160 acres of free farmland or a municipal town lot, simply by being the very first person to reach the parcel and drive a wooden stake into the prairie soil.

23
00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:19.080
[George] By the morning of April 22, an estimated 50,000 men, women, and children had massed along the northern border near Arkansas City, Kansas, and the southern border along the Canadian River near Purcell.

24
00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:24.920
[George] It was the most extraordinary, colorful and desperate assembly in American frontier history.

25
00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:32.120
[George] For miles along the boundary, camps of wagons, horses, and makeshift tents stretched as far as the eye could see.

26
00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:44.480
[George] Gambling tables operated in the dust, traveling preachers held prayer meetings on tailgates, and vendors sold canteens, surveyors' twine, and wooden corner stakes at exorbitant prices.

27
00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:49.440
[George] Lined up side by side were Union and Confederate veterans in faded uniforms.

28
00:03:49.680 --> 00:04:04.320
[George] Recently arrived immigrants from Germany, Ireland, and Scandinavia, cowboys in leather chaps, bankers in black three-piece suits, young farm couples with swaddled infants, and single women seeking independent land ownership.

29
00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:08.080
[George] They were equipped with every imaginable mode of transportation.

30
00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:21.920
[George] Prized Kentucky thoroughbred racehorses, sturdy draft mules, buckboard wagons, prairie schooners, bicycles, unicycles, and thousands of impoverished laborers on foot carrying cedar stakes and canteen flasks.

31
00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:33.600
[George] Along the Santa Fe Railroad line, passenger trains with fifteen cars each sat hissing on the tracks, crammed with thousands of passengers hanging from the windows, platforms and roofs.

32
00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:44.320
[George] Mounted United States cavalry troopers from the 5th Cavalry patrolled the perimeter line with drawn carbines, under strict military orders to shoot anyone who crossed early.

33
00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:54.640
[George] The heat was sweltering, the prairie wind whipped up blinding red dust, and fifty thousand competitors watched the cavalry officers repeatedly check their pocket watches.

34
00:04:54.640 --> 00:05:02.320
[George] At precisely twelve o'clock noon, a cavalry officer drew his service revolver and fired a shot into the cloudless sky.

35
00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:10.880
[George] A split second later, brass army bugles blared across miles of open prairie, and a field cannon boomed along the southern line at Purcell.

36
00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:14.480
[George] The border erupted in a deafening, thunderous roar.

37
00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:20.480
[George] Fifty thousand pioneers surged forward across the landscape in a simultaneous, wild charge.

38
00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:25.280
[George] It was an avalanche of horse flesh, iron wheels, and human desperation.

39
00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:32.600
[George] A massive, suffocating cloud of red dust rose hundreds of feet into the air, completely blotting out the midday sun.

40
00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:37.960
[George] Wagons collided at full gallop, flipping over and splintering into firewood.

41
00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:50.120
[George] Horses stepped into concealed prairie dog burrows, throwing riders headfirst onto the hard clay, and pedestrians on foot sprinted forward with wild eyes, clutching flags and surveyors' pegs.

42
00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:58.120
[George] Equestrians on swift thoroughbreds pulled away from the heavy farm wagons, leaping dry gullies and splashing through spring creeks.

43
00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:13.240
[George] The passenger trains crawled forward at exactly eight miles per hour, as mandated by federal regulations, while eager homesteaders leapt recklessly from open coach windows into brushy ravines and creek beds to stake their claims ahead of the locomotive.

44
00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:19.960
[George] While tens of thousands risked life and limb in the legitimate race, thousands of others had already cheated.

45
00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:27.320
[George] These were the infamous Sooners, individuals who had slipped past the cavalry patrols under cover of darkness the night before.

46
00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:39.240
[George] Hiding themselves and their horses in dense willow thickets, limestone ravines, and caves along Soldier Creek and Crutchow Creek near the prime water sources and prospective railroad depots.

47
00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:48.840
[George] Many Sooners were deputy federal marshals, railroad employees, and government surveyors who used their official access to scout the landscape in advance.

48
00:06:48.840 --> 00:07:02.360
[George] When legitimate racers rode their foaming, exhausted thoroughbreds into Guthrie and Oklahoma City around 12:30 that afternoon, they were enraged to find men casually lounging under oak trees on the finest quarter sections.

49
00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:13.080
[George] These sooners were sitting beside smoking campfires, drinking coffee, with horses that were completely cool, dry and rested, and water wells already half dug.

50
00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:23.120
[George] Furious shouting matches and violent brawls erupted instantly across the prairie, with Colt revolvers and Winchester rifles drawn over disputed surveyors' stakes.

51
00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:29.680
[George] Several Sooners were shot dead on their claims by exhausted, furious racers who caught them in the act of fraud.

52
00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:41.440
[George] The bitter legal battles over sooner perjury and counterfeit filings would overwhelm Oklahoma territorial courtrooms for more than twenty years, eventually reaching the United States Supreme Court.

53
00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:50.400
[George] Despite the rampant cheating and chaos, what unfolded between noon and sunset was an unprecedented miracle of American frontier urbanization.

54
00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:59.440
[George] At twelve o'clock noon, Guthrie and Oklahoma City were empty, rolling expanses of wild prairie grass without a single permanent structure.

55
00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:05.840
[George] By four o'clock that afternoon, both settlements boasted populations exceeding 10,000 people.

56
00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:11.840
[George] Tens of thousands of white canvas wall tents sprouted across the landscape like mushroom fields.

57
00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:22.240
[George] By nightfall, provisional municipal governments had been organized, temporary marshals appointed, and main streets mapped out with surveyors' twine and cedar posts.

58
00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:25.600
[George] Commercial enterprise sprang up instantaneously.

59
00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:39.840
[George] Bankers conducted deposits from the backboards of overturned wagons, attorneys drafted deeds on tree stumps by lantern light, saloon keepers sold whiskey from barrels, and dry goods merchants laid out bolts of cloth on blankets.

60
00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:52.400
[George] Wells were dug by torchlight, campfires illuminated miles of prairie, and the very first edition of the Guthrie Daily News was printed on a hand press and sold on the dirt street by eight o'clock that evening.

61
00:08:52.400 --> 00:09:00.000
[George] In a single six-hour span, two million acres of raw frontier wilderness had been transformed into an organized commonwealth.

62
00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:07.960
[George] Yet, behind the thrilling romantic legend of pioneer heroism lay a profound and enduring historical tragedy.

63
00:09:07.960 --> 00:09:19.240
[George] The unassigned lands had originally been guaranteed to the Creek and Seminole nations in sacred federal treaties, pledging that the soil would belong to them as long as grass grows and water runs.

64
00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:28.440
[George] Following the Civil War, the federal government confiscated these central tracts under the pretext of punishing tribes that had allied with the Confederacy.

65
00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:34.680
[George] The April 22nd land rush was merely the opening wedge in the complete dismantling of Indian territory.

66
00:09:34.680 --> 00:09:50.760
[George] Over the next 18 years, through subsequent land runs, including the massive 1893 run into the Cherokee Outlet and the coercive allotment process of the Dawes Commission, tribal communal lands were systematically liquidated and absorbed by white settlers.

67
00:09:50.760 --> 00:10:01.640
[George] The independent tribal governments of the five civilized tribes were formally dissolved, culminating in the admission of Oklahoma as the 46th American state in 1907.

68
00:10:01.640 --> 00:10:15.080
[George] The Oklahoma land rush remains a defining symbol of American expansion, an intoxicating collision of boundless human ambition, reckless pioneer grit, and the unyielding price of manifest destiny.

69
00:10:30.040 --> 00:10:33.000
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

70
00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:34.280
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

71
00:10:34.280 --> 00:10:37.560
[Rebecca] Join us next time for another forgotten chapter of history.

72
00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:40.040
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

